# x204 — The Trust Protocol for Agentic Commerce

**HTTP-native, identity-authenticated, facilitator-free micropayments for autonomous agents.**

x204 unifies what every other protocol handles separately: identity, trust, payment, and reputation
become a single atomic operation. No facilitator. No signup. No middleman. One HTTP call.

The name: HTTP 204 = "No Content" → no middleman, no centralized authority, no unnecessary overhead.

---

## 1. WHY x204 EXISTS

### What x402 Got Right
- HTTP-native payment headers (PAYMENT-REQUIRED, PAYMENT-SIGNATURE)
- EIP-3009 / Permit2 signed authorizations (cryptographic, on-chain)
- Open source (Apache 2.0) — anyone can implement
- Proven on Base L2 with USDC micropayments

### What x402 Gets Wrong

| Gap                      | Impact                                                        |
|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| No identity              | Server doesn't know WHO is paying — just that someone paid    |
| No trust/reputation      | A scam agent and a reliable agent pay the same price          |
| No mutual authentication | Client can't cryptographically verify the SERVER              |
| No quality guarantees    | You pay, you get *something*, no proof of quality             |
| No portable receipts     | Receipts are siloed to each platform                          |
| Facilitator dependency   | Can't settle without a centralized intermediary               |
| No feedback loop         | Zero mechanism for quality reporting to improve the system    |
| No onboarding flow       | Requires wallet + USDC + facilitator before first query       |

### The x204 Thesis

> Trust replaces infrastructure. If both parties have cryptographically verifiable identities
> with portable trust histories, you don't need escrow, you don't need facilitators, you don't
> even need immediate payment. Trust becomes the settlement layer — just like credit scores
> enable deferred payment in the real economy, but for AI agents with cryptographic proofs.

